#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86dd1d70-9e6f-59be-849d-72be84c7adc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163821} {:answer/id #uuid "87f23450-c1d7-5124-bdb4-ac35058cb519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163822} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef62159-b31d-5bb0-b077-3e5137dca3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163823} {:answer/id #uuid "4cec2c5e-c9b8-54d8-b289-864269c4c6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163824} {:answer/id #uuid "79b366c9-66ad-5b4e-a748-daf073412964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79b366c9-66ad-5b4e-a748-daf073412964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163825} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87884de-5b9a-505d-bf36-764539d5ee39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "018ba806-567b-5332-8cf5-cb28969716b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161158" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+3y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{14}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+3y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+3y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,4x+6y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(-2x-3y+4x+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+3xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb85ba7-210f-564f-9e9f-8552c2d35560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366602} {:answer/id #uuid "073798a1-6b92-5611-b70a-76e7c0b692dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366603} {:answer/id #uuid "ed19b014-3dfe-5988-a2b2-db5392654afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366604} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b3e57d-7eec-562b-a2d8-88261bbb1678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366605} {:answer/id #uuid "c7905471-7089-5b0e-a58c-5381d2c5bf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb85ba7-210f-564f-9e9f-8552c2d35560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366602} :answer-field/id #uuid "803b991c-eae9-5c86-a8af-24ba02361d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "319662ec-c01c-5bc5-9295-3611e5d3afa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161187" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97160bbe-9225-587d-84d6-6af779e74f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366609} {:answer/id #uuid "e88a5580-5343-5117-99fb-d85fd9b7f5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366610} {:answer/id #uuid "79fd1428-07b4-5082-ac2a-6536a2b8727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366611} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b1b08e-1d05-523c-b86f-a5b05f295ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366612} {:answer/id #uuid "8b990648-99d5-5958-a6e8-523415e858e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97160bbe-9225-587d-84d6-6af779e74f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366609} :answer-field/id #uuid "a009f081-1a97-54b6-880f-9c71ee87926c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83d2d64f-ef6c-54fd-8a2d-e9caec911649" :question/math-academy-id "q-161195" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-2y+1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61cfc99-111f-5f0f-a4f3-b0cf0ef49716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366616} {:answer/id #uuid "6f95f533-4021-5306-87e7-0c30edb155c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366617} {:answer/id #uuid "2993db3b-1f8a-5445-a7c3-97ccdfb158ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366618} {:answer/id #uuid "56439c31-61a7-5245-9ce7-83f18c3171d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366619} {:answer/id #uuid "1912c796-b407-53e2-88a2-6bc2cd13add7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56439c31-61a7-5245-9ce7-83f18c3171d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366619} :answer-field/id #uuid "35be2174-3427-5dd8-9d58-5e8811fb2c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b49439b2-a074-558f-9e48-de199d0a401a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b137c1-6170-55d8-9953-d0a8c1b1b7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366623} {:answer/id #uuid "5528c82a-1aba-5b62-95b2-c3453eaff52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366624} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb1197a-f48b-5bba-a55b-63af6b6b0c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366625} {:answer/id #uuid "cc61d927-b49a-50ee-a68e-d42ce868fe75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366626} {:answer/id #uuid "bd33d004-f78c-53f0-89f6-6d3db366b20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc61d927-b49a-50ee-a68e-d42ce868fe75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366626} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb79bbb-124a-5f13-b63f-e0a26d3e2b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c160107-8805-58b0-9a37-d31f8eeb4e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-162551" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
